examples.solution_text:
Use degree mode. Cosine gives the horizontal coordinate and sine gives the vertical coordinate.

$\begin{align*}x&=\cos145^{\circ}\\x&\approx-0.82\\y&=\sin145^{\circ}\\y&\approx0.57\end{align*}$

So $P\approx(-0.82,0.57)$. The signs agree with a point left of the $y$-axis and above the $x$-axis.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=22mm,y=22mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[<->] (-1.3,0)--(1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\coordinate (P) at (145:1);
\draw (0,0)--(P);
\draw[dashed] (P)--({cos(145)},0);
\draw[->] (0.3,0) arc (0:145:0.3);
\path (72.5:0.51) node {$145^{\circ}$};
\fill[black] (P) circle (1.3pt);
\path (P) node[above left] {$P\approx(-0.82,0.57)$};
\path (0,0) node[below right] {$O$};
\path (1,0) node[below] {$1$};
\path (0,-1) node[left] {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The radius to $P$ makes an angle of $255^{\circ}$ anticlockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Which coordinate gives $\sin255^{\circ}$, and which gives $\cos255^{\circ}$? Explain the sign of each.

