\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
At $0^{\circ}$, the radius points along the positive $x$-axis. A full turn returns it to the same position.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=22mm,y=22mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[<->] (-1.3,0)--(1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(1,0);
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.3pt);
\path (1,0) node[above right] {$P(1,0)$};
\path (0,0) node[below left] {$O$};
\path (-1,0) node[below] {$-1$};
\path (0,1) node[left] {$1$};
\path (0,-1) node[left] {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For both angles, $P=(1,0)$. Read cosine first and sine second:

$\begin{align*}\cos0^{\circ}&=1\\\sin0^{\circ}&=0\\\cos360^{\circ}&=1\\\sin360^{\circ}&=0\end{align*}$

Cosine gives the $x$-coordinate and sine gives the $y$-coordinate, so $P=(0,-1)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=22mm,y=22mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[<->] (-1.3,0)--(1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(0,-1);
\draw[->] (0.3,0) arc (0:270:0.3);
\path (135:0.52) node {$270^{\circ}$};
\fill[black] (0,-1) circle (1.3pt);
\path (0,-1) node[below left] {$P(0,-1)$};
\path (1,0) node[below] {$1$};
\path (0,1) node[left] {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

This is the bottom of the unit circle, reached after three quarters of an anticlockwise turn. Therefore $\theta=270^{\circ}$.
